Title: Hermeticity guard does not cover Python test suites
Created: 2026-09-16
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-09-16T13:24Z claude created

--- body ---
## Context

`tests/test_bash_tests_are_hermetic.sh` (0359) makes BASH_ENV hermeticity
mechanical — but it is a textual scanner over shell source, discovered by a
`tests/*.sh` glob. Its own header is explicit that coverage is stated rather
than implied, and it never mentions Python.

**20 `tests/*.py` suites spawn subprocesses without clearing `BASH_ENV`.** A
Python child gets `BASH_ENV` from `os.environ` exactly as a shell child does, so
the 2026-07-27 defect class is live on the other half of the test tree: an
injected fixture credential is overridden by the real one at child startup, and
a failing assertion prints what it found. That incident leaked two live keys and
forced a rotation.

Same remedy, other language: pass an env with `BASH_ENV` cleared (or `env -i`)
to every `subprocess.run`/`Popen`, and ratchet it with a guard over `tests/*.py`.

Sibling of 0939 (xtrace tracing of the same loader), which covers a different
exposure path of the same mechanism.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] A guard covers `tests/*.py` subprocess spawns for `BASH_ENV` neutralization
- [ ] It reports file names and line numbers only, never the offending text and
      never an environment value, per 0359's own discipline
- [ ] It states its coverage and blind spots explicitly, and fails loudly rather
      than reporting clean a file it could not parse
- [ ] It carries a static negative control that fails against a naive implementation
- [ ] The 20 existing offenders are fixed, verified by a fake sentinel, never a real key
- [ ] `make check` passes
